package changes

import "github.com/yusing/mekugi/contracts/command"

var Command = command.Definition[Service]{Name: "mchanges", Description: "Review, revert, or reapply captured filesystem changes from stock apply_patch, shell file operations, and supported Python/JS writes; not a Git diff or provisional preview. Usage: `mchanges --list [ID[..ID] ...] [--workspace DIR] [--max-tokens N] | mchanges [--mine | ID[..ID] ...] [--summary|--history] [--workspace DIR] [--max-tokens N] [-- PATH ...] | mchanges revert|apply ID[..ID] ... [--workspace DIR] [--max-tokens N] [-- PATH ...]`. Bare mchanges, --mine, and --list without IDs are scoped to the calling thread, not whole-workspace coverage; --list compresses the caller's IDs with counts; --summary shows diff-pane file statuses and per-path counts. For composed review, use `mchanges ID...` or bare `mchanges` for your own captures. This default composes selected captured diffs in capture order, not a live Git diff. Use --history only to diagnose individual attempts, original input, host results and capture diagnostics. `revert` undoes and `apply` replays selected changes in the workspace, git-style: drifted regions merge; overlapping edits leave conflict markers (exit 1). Output states each file relative to mchanges history, not Git (`clean`, `+N -N`, `UU` conflict, `??` unknown). Read modes continue past failed targets, keeping available results on stdout and errors on stderr with nonzero status; apply and revert retain dependency checks. Recorded diffs are historical evidence, not proof of current workspace contents. The revert is itself recorded as a change. Default token budget: 4000. Commands support shell pipes, for example `mchanges amber1..amber5 | git apply --cached -p0`. For patch export, collect all pages, require a successful read and validate the collected output before applying it; pure moves use display-only headers. Piping partial stdout can modify the index even when mchanges fails.", Run: Service.Execute}
